    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d880">

                <pb facs="0951" n="1785" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1785"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111N83/45</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schoenfeld</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-09-03T16:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 3, 1918, 4 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>450. Your 1085, August 31, 5 p.m.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d880-f1"/> Norwegian
                    Minister presented note dated August 20<anchor n="2" xml:id="d880-f2"/> under
                    the instructions of his Government reporting certain instances where foreign
                    submarines have passed through or stopped within the territorial waters of
                    Norway and requesting an investigation with a view to ascertaining whether any
                        <pb facs="0952" n="1786" xml:id="pg1786"/> American submarines have violated
                    the provisions of the Royal decree of January 30, 1917.</p>
                <p>A further note was addressed to the Department by the Minister dated August
                        29<anchor n="1" xml:id="d880-f3"/> presenting further instances where
                    foreign submarines have been seen within the territorial waters of Norway and
                    adding that the Norwegian Government “who learns with an ever increasing anxiety
                    of each new case where the Royal Norwegian decree of January 30, 1917 is
                    violated, and who cannot help feeling some impatience whilst awaiting the
                    replies from the belligerent Governments to the previous note, thinks that it
                    ought by these presents to advise these Governments that while it has already
                    increased the inspection in the territorial waters concerned, it will deem
                    itself compelled to take special military measures in these waters in order to
                    maintain its rights.”</p>
                <p>The Department is today replying to the Norwegian Minister, and requesting him to
                    so advise his Government, that no submarine of the United States Navy has ever
                    entered the territorial waters of Norway.</p>
                <p>The foregoing is for your information and may be communicated informally to the
                    Minister of Foreign Affairs, if you so desire, in anticipation of the Norwegian
                    Minister’s communication to his Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
